/* Security+ SY0-801 content (from the IT Field Manual) */
const SP={"OBJ":[{"name":"1.0 General Security Concepts","items":[{"id":"sp11","code":"1.1","t":"Security concepts and controls","ask":true,"d":"CIA triad, AAA, non-repudiation, Zero Trust, least privilege, defense in depth. Control categories: technical, managerial, operational, physical. Control types: preventive, deterring, detective, corrective, compensating, directive.","hook":"Job-site version: a guardrail is preventive, a \"hard hats required\" sign is directive, the camera is detective, and the incident report is corrective.","lab":"Lab 2 (each GPO is a technical, preventive control) and Lab 6 (alerts are detective)."},{"id":"sp12","code":"1.2","t":"Change management","ask":true,"d":"Change advisory board and approvals, ownership, stakeholders, impact analysis, test results, backout plan, fail forward, maintenance windows, SOPs. Technical side: allow/deny lists, restricted activities, downtime, service and app restarts, legacy apps, dependencies. Updating diagrams and procedures. Version control.","lab":"Your VM snapshots are your backout plan. GitHub is your version control. The Lab 7 runbook is an SOP."},{"id":"sp13","code":"1.3","t":"Cryptographic solutions","ask":true,"d":"PKI: public and private keys, key escrow. Certificates: CAs, CRL, OCSP, self-signed, third-party, root of trust, CSR, wildcard. Encryption: symmetric vs asymmetric; full disk, partition, file, volume, database, record; transport, key exchange, algorithms, key length. Digital signatures, salting, hashing, obfuscation.","lab":"Lab 8 (your own certificate authority and BitLocker)."}]},{"name":"2.0 Threats, Vulnerabilities, and Attacks","items":[{"id":"sp21","code":"2.1","t":"Threats and vulnerabilities","ask":true,"d":"Threat feeds, likelihood, impact, intelligence sources, threat life cycle. Vulnerability scoring with CVSS, CVE IDs, prioritization, vulnerability types.","lab":"Look up the CVE and CVSS score behind three of your Lab 5 SCC findings on the NVD site."},{"id":"sp22","code":"2.2","t":"Threat actors and motivations","ask":true,"d":"Actors: organized crime, terrorist, unskilled attacker, hacktivist, insider, accidental, competitor, state-sponsored. Motivations: financial, influence, IP theft, notoriety, espionage, chaos, extortion, curiosity, revenge, ideological, political, ethical. Attributes: internal/external, funding, sophistication.","lab":"Flashcards. Match each actor to its most likely motivation."},{"id":"sp23","code":"2.3","t":"Threat vectors and sources","ask":true,"d":"Message-based (email, SMS, RCS, IM, collaboration tools), image-based (QR codes, CAPTCHA), attachments (macros, RTF, PDF), browser (extensions, cookies, session tokens), network devices, remote access (RDP, VNC, VPN), endpoints and living-off-the-land tools, supply chain (MSPs, SaaS, logistics), malicious USB, human (impersonation, contractor, visitor, watering hole), IoT, OT, physical (locks, access vestibules, passes), signal (Bluetooth, RF, NFC).","lab":"Lab 6: catch a living-off-the-land command (encoded PowerShell) in your SIEM."},{"id":"sp24","code":"2.4","t":"Vulnerabilities and attack surfaces","ask":true,"d":"Unsupported, unpatched, obsolete and unmanaged systems; open ports and services; race conditions (TOC/TOU); malicious updates; hardcoded secrets; unsafe exception handling; OS and virtualization flaws; zero-days; weak crypto; stale credentials; rogue devices; shadow IT; wireless; LLMs; identity providers; mobile; misconfiguration; public repos and object storage.","lab":"Lab 5 SCC findings, plus an nmap scan of your own lab network."},{"id":"sp25","code":"2.5","t":"Indicators of malicious activity","ask":true,"d":"Malware (ransomware, trojan, worm, spyware, rootkit, keylogger, logic bomb, fileless). Physical (tailgating, shoulder surfing, skimming). Network (DDoS, downgrade, rogue devices, sniffing, spoofing, on-path, DNS attacks, cache poisoning). Social engineering (phishing, spear phishing, whaling, smishing, vishing, quishing, deepfakes). IoCs (hashes, IPs, domains, malicious processes, log manipulation, account lockout, impossible travel, concurrent sessions). App attacks (injection, buffer overflow, replay, privilege escalation, forgery, directory traversal). Credential attacks (spraying, brute force, user enumeration, MFA bypass).","lab":"Lock out a lab user with bad passwords and find event ID 4740 on DC01. Your Bash script's failed-login check counts too."},{"id":"sp26","code":"2.6","t":"AI threats and vulnerabilities","ask":true,"d":"Model manipulation, poisoning, prompt injection, data loss, bias, explainability, hallucinations, jailbreaking, evasion, privacy, ethics, session hijacking, code execution.","lab":"Your own GHOSTSTACK and ANVIL work. Explain why you put an isolation layer in front of untrusted code execution."}]},{"name":"3.0 Security Architecture","items":[{"id":"sp31","code":"3.1","t":"Architecture models","ask":true,"d":"Cloud (serverless, multicloud, hybrid, private, public, community, IaC), OT, on-prem, air-gapped networks, microservices, logical and physical segmentation. Technical considerations: availability, resilience, open source vs proprietary, responsibility, power, ease of recovery. Business: data sovereignty, classification, cost, scalability, risk."},{"id":"sp32","code":"3.2","t":"Protecting infrastructure","ask":true,"d":"Device placement, security zones, attack surface, diversity. Zero Trust: user authentication, device health and inventory, app access control. Secure access: VPN, tunneling, out-of-band management, SSE, end-to-end encrypted messaging, file transfer. Identity: gMSAs, least-privilege accounts, privilege creep. Failure modes (fail-open vs fail-closed).","lab":"Lab 4 (Users and Servers zones in pfSense) and Lab 2 (create one gMSA)."},{"id":"sp33","code":"3.3","t":"Protecting data","ask":true,"d":"Structured vs unstructured. Data at rest, in use, in transit. Classifications (public, sensitive, confidential, restricted, critical, secret, top secret). Methods: masking, hashing, filtering, tokenization, encryption, transposition, deidentification, obfuscation. Roles: owner, custodian, steward, operator, controller, subprocessor. Marking and labeling, geofencing, data location. Life cycle: creation to disposal. Compliance: health, personal, financial, child, IP, legal data.","hook":"Your DoD CUI training is this objective from the user's side: marking, handling, and disposal."},{"id":"sp34","code":"3.4","t":"Resilience and recovery","ask":true,"d":"Hot, warm and cold sites. Platform diversity. Load balancing, clustering, autoscaling, high availability. Power: UPS, redundant PSUs, generators, surge protection. Backups: retention, immutability, scope, restoration testing. Testing: failover, simulation, parallel processing. DR, BC, capacity planning. Metrics: RTO, RPO, MTTR, MTBF.","lab":"Lab 7: time your restore. That number is your real RTO."}]},{"name":"4.0 Security Operations","items":[{"id":"sp41","code":"4.1","t":"Mitigating controls","ask":true,"d":"Segmentation, access controls, hardening, sandboxing. Deception: honeypots, honeynets, honeyfiles, honeytokens, canary accounts. Monitoring, MDM, app allow/block lists. NIDS/NIPS, HIDS/HIPS, WIPS. Firewalls: rate limiting, WAF, rule-based, UTM, Layer 4 vs Layer 7. Content filters, DLP, proxies. EDR, XDR, antivirus. NAC: captive portals, 802.1X, posture. Secrets scanning. App security: input validation, secure cookies, static analysis, code signing. Email: DMARC, SPF, DKIM, BIMI. OS: Group Policy, SELinux.","lab":"Labs 2, 4, 5 and 6. Bonus: make a canary account in AD that should never log in, and alert on it."},{"id":"sp42","code":"4.2","t":"Asset management","ask":true,"d":"Asset life cycle: planning and scoping, acquisition and procurement, assignment and accounting, monitoring and tracking, disposal and decommissioning.","lab":"Keep an inventory of every lab VM: name, OS, IP, owner, purpose, date built."},{"id":"sp43","code":"4.3","t":"Vulnerability management","ask":true,"d":"Identification: scanning, IPAM, CSPM, source code review. Prioritization: severity, pen test report review. Remediation, verification, reporting (internal, external, bug bounties, responsible disclosure).","lab":"Lab 5 follows this cycle exactly: scan, prioritize, fix, rescan, report."},{"id":"sp44","code":"4.4","t":"Alerting and monitoring","ask":true,"d":"Monitoring systems, apps, infrastructure. Log aggregation, alerting, scanning, archiving, reporting, alert tuning. Tools: benchmarks, agents vs agentless, SIEM, AV, DLP, vulnerability scanners, orchestration, packet analyzers. Protocols: NetFlow, SNMP, syslog, SCAP. Automated alerts, port mirroring, dashboards, network management systems.","lab":"Labs 5 and 6."},{"id":"sp45","code":"4.5","t":"Identity and access management","ask":true,"d":"Provisioning and deprovisioning, permissions, identity proofing, federation, SSO (SAML, LDAP, OAuth). Account types: user, privileged (global, local), service, third-party, emergency access. MFA: hard and soft tokens, biometrics, OTP, backup codes. Access models: rule, role, time-based, mandatory, discretionary, just-in-time. Access reviews. Passkeys, passwordless, password managers, password policy (length, complexity, reuse, expiration, age).","lab":"Labs 2 and 3: create, change, disable, and delete accounts the way a help desk does."},{"id":"sp46","code":"4.6","t":"Automation and orchestration","ask":true,"d":"Use cases: user and resource provisioning, desired state, anomaly detection, ticket management. Considerations: guardrails, logic, complexity, cost, process risk, deployment. AI: agentic, chatbots, predictive analysis, AI-augmented baselines. Outcomes: efficiency, enforced baselines, less downtime. SecOps: CI/CD. Workflows and integrations.","lab":"Your CSV bulk-user script and both health-check scripts."},{"id":"sp47","code":"4.7","t":"Incident response","ask":true,"d":"Preparation (training, testing, tabletops, playbooks, simulations, roles). Identification (detection, advisories, threat hunting). Investigation (forensics, chain of custody, e-discovery, preservation). Containment (quarantine, isolation). Negotiation. Eradication and recovery. Notification (stakeholders, customers, law enforcement, mandatory reporting). Post-incident (lessons learned, root cause, PIR).","lab":"After Lab 6, write a one-page incident report on your test alert, using these phases as headings."},{"id":"sp48","code":"4.8","t":"Investigation data sources","ask":true,"d":"Log types: access (logical and physical), device, server, application, authentication, communication, audit, endpoint, network, metadata. Sources: vulnerability scans, automated reports, NetFlow/IPFIX, surveillance footage, security tools, dashboards, packet captures. File and log integrity. System images: memory dumps, bit-level copies, snapshots. Stakeholders: HR, accounts, legal. Log parsing.","lab":"Get-WinEvent on DC01, grep on auth.log, and a Wireshark capture of your own lab traffic."}]},{"name":"5.0 Program Management and Oversight","items":[{"id":"sp51","code":"5.1","t":"Governance, risk, and compliance artifacts","ask":true,"d":"Guidelines (benchmarks, advisories, implementation guides, reference architecture). Standards (baselines, passwords, physical security, RFCs, encryption). Procedures (SOPs, runbooks). Plans (BC, DR). Policies (BYOD, AUP, clean desk, infosec, IR, data classification and retention, access control, disposal, vulnerability disclosure, privacy).","hook":"A STIG is a benchmark. Your Lab 7 runbook is a procedure."},{"id":"sp52","code":"5.2","t":"Risk management","ask":true,"d":"Identification (assets, owners). Assessment (scoring, categorization). Analysis (impact, likelihood, owner, current mitigations, qualitative vs quantitative). Risk register. Treatment: transfer, accept, avoid, mitigate. BIA, risk appetite, residual risk, regulatory and legal factors.","hook":"Memorize: ALE = SLE × ARO. If a $10,000 server fails twice a year, ALE = $20,000."},{"id":"sp53","code":"5.3","t":"Third-party risk","ask":true,"d":"Vendor selection: RFP, RFI, RFQ, EOI, due diligence, conflict of interest. Agreements: SLA, SLO, MOU, MOA, NDA, MSA, SOW. Monitoring: right to audit, SLA monitoring, vendor registry, assessments, attestations, pen testing. Constraints: staffing, jurisdiction, ROI, vendor lock-in. Rules of engagement.","hook":"You work for a contractor on a base. Tribal One's contract with the Air Force is the vendor side of this objective."},{"id":"sp54","code":"5.4","t":"Security compliance","ask":true,"d":"Compliance training (data handling, AML/CTF, anti-bribery). Monitoring (attestations, acknowledgements). Consequences (reputation, fines, legal, contractual, sanctions, loss of license). Privacy (right to be forgotten, opt-in/out, data correction, processing limits, controller vs processor, ownership). Legal holds, legal orders, retention requirements."},{"id":"sp55","code":"5.5","t":"Audits and assessments","ask":true,"d":"Data gathering: sampling, surveys, interviews, assertions, MITRE ATT&CK, Cyber Kill Chain, Diamond Model. Scoping: audit charter, frequency. Engagements: gap analysis, internal (compliance, audit committee, self-assessment), external (exams, regulatory, independent audits), benchmarking. Pen testing: known, unknown, partially known; physical, offensive, defensive, integrated; passive vs active recon. Frameworks and standards. Functional and behavioral testing."},{"id":"sp56","code":"5.6","t":"Security awareness","ask":true,"d":"Training types: onboarding, ongoing, targeted, corrective. Delivery: LMS, self-service portals, one-to-one, one-to-many. Effectiveness: metrics, managerial reports, behavior risk scoring. Topics: social engineering, passwords, remote work, BYOD, BEC, removable media, situational awareness, OPSEC.","hook":"Your DoD OPSEC, Antiterrorism and CI awareness courses are examples of this objective."}]},{"name":"Exam readiness","items":[{"id":"spacr","t":"Acronym deck in Anki","d":"Build cards from the acronym list at the end of the official objectives PDF. Review them on commutes.","steps":[["spacr1","Deck built"],["spacr2","All cards reviewed once"],["spacr3","Daily reviews for 2 weeks straight"]]},{"id":"sppx","t":"Practice exams at 85% or better","d":"Use SY0-801 practice exams only. Review every missed question and write down why the right answer is right.","steps":[["sppx1","Practice exam 1 at 85%+"],["sppx2","Practice exam 2 at 85%+"],["sppx3","Practice exam 3 at 85%+"]]},{"id":"spbook","t":"Book and pass the exam","d":"Confirm the V8 price on CompTIA's site and check whether Strayer will count Security+ toward your remaining credits.","links":[["CompTIA Security+ V8","https://www.comptia.org/en/certifications/security/v8/"]],"steps":[["spbook1","Voucher bought"],["spbook2","Exam booked"],["spbook3","Passed"]]}]}],"QB":[{"id":"q11a","o":"1.1","q":"A sign on the server room door reads \"Authorized personnel only.\" Which control type is this?","c":["Directive","Preventive","Detective","Compensating"],"e":"It tells people what to do but doesn't physically stop anyone. A locked door would be preventive."},{"id":"q11b","o":"1.1","q":"A legacy system can't support MFA, so the team isolates it on its own VLAN and adds extra monitoring. What kind of control is that?","c":["Compensating","Corrective","Deterrent","Directive"],"e":"Compensating controls stand in for a required control that can't be implemented."},{"id":"q11c","o":"1.1","q":"A user denies sending an email, but it carries their digital signature. Which concept proves they sent it?","c":["Non-repudiation","Availability","Authorization","Least privilege"],"e":"Non-repudiation means the sender can't credibly deny the action, because only their private key could have signed it."},{"id":"q12a","o":"1.2","q":"Before a firewall change, the team writes down exactly how to undo it if the business app breaks. What is this?","c":["Backout plan","Impact analysis","Fail forward","Standard operating procedure"],"e":"A backout plan is the documented way to reverse a change. Your VM snapshots play this role in your lab."},{"id":"q12b","o":"1.2","q":"An approved change requires restarting DNS on the domain controllers. When should it happen?","c":["During the approved maintenance window","As soon as the CAB submits it","When users start reporting problems","Any time, since a DNS restart is quick"],"e":"Changes that cause downtime or service restarts go in a maintenance window so the impact is planned."},{"id":"q13a","o":"1.3","q":"A browser needs to check whether a certificate was revoked, in real time, without downloading the whole revocation list. What does it use?","c":["OCSP","CRL","CSR","Key escrow"],"e":"OCSP answers \"is this one certificate still good?\" A CRL is the full downloaded list."},{"id":"q13b","o":"1.3","q":"An admin wants one certificate that covers www, mail, and vpn under example.mil. Which type fits?","c":["Wildcard","Self-signed","Code-signing","Root CA"],"e":"A wildcard (*.example.mil) covers every first-level subdomain."},{"id":"q13c","o":"1.3","q":"Two users pick the same password, but their stored hashes are different. What made the difference?","c":["Salting","Obfuscation","Tokenization","Masking"],"e":"A salt is random data added to each password before hashing, so identical passwords hash differently."},{"id":"q13d","o":"1.3","q":"Which uses the SAME key to encrypt and decrypt?","c":["Symmetric encryption","Asymmetric encryption","Digital signatures","Hashing"],"e":"Symmetric (like AES) uses one shared key. Asymmetric uses a public/private pair. Hashing is one-way."},{"id":"q21a","o":"2.1","q":"Which gives a vulnerability a numeric severity score from 0 to 10?","c":["CVSS","CVE","CWE","MITRE ATT&CK"],"e":"CVSS scores severity. CVE is the ID number for a specific vulnerability."},{"id":"q21b","o":"2.1","q":"Organizations subscribe to shared lists of malicious IPs, domains, and hashes. What are these?","c":["Threat feeds","Vulnerability scans","Penetration tests","Audit logs"],"e":"Threat feeds share indicators of compromise between organizations."},{"id":"q22a","o":"2.2","q":"A well-funded group spends months quietly stealing designs from defense contractors. Which actor fits best?","c":["State-sponsored","Hacktivist","Unskilled attacker","Competitor"],"e":"Big resources, patience, and espionage point to a nation-state (often called an APT)."},{"id":"q22b","o":"2.2","q":"A fired employee uses an account nobody disabled to delete project files. Which actor and motivation fit?","c":["Insider, revenge","Hacktivist, ideological","Organized crime, financial","Unskilled attacker, notoriety"],"e":"They had inside access and acted out of anger. This is also why deprovisioning (4.5) matters."},{"id":"q23a","o":"2.3","q":"Attackers compromise a forum that the target company's engineers visit often, hoping to infect them. What is this?","c":["Watering hole","Whaling","Vishing","Smishing"],"e":"Like poisoning the watering hole animals visit, attackers infect a site the target already trusts."},{"id":"q23b","o":"2.3","q":"An attacker uses PowerShell and certutil, which are already on the machine, to avoid antivirus. What is this called?","c":["Living off the land","Supply chain attack","Rogue device","Shadow IT"],"e":"Living-off-the-land attacks use built-in tools, so nothing new gets installed. Lab 6 catches one."},{"id":"q24a","o":"2.4","q":"A developer pushes code to a public GitHub repo with a cloud API key in it. Which vulnerability is this?","c":["Hardcoded secrets","Race condition","Zero-day","Unsupported product"],"e":"Secrets in code get found fast in public repos. Secrets scanning (4.1) catches this."},{"id":"q24b","o":"2.4","q":"A program checks a file's permissions, then opens it a moment later. An attacker swaps the file in between. What is this?","c":["Race condition (time-of-check/time-of-use)","Buffer overflow","Replay attack","SQL injection"],"e":"The gap between the check (TOC) and the use (TOU) is the race."},{"id":"q24c","o":"2.4","q":"An employee sets up their own Wi-Fi router under their desk without IT knowing. What is this best called?","c":["Rogue device","Zero-day","Misconfigured IdP","Malicious update"],"e":"An unauthorized device on the network is a rogue device. Using unapproved tools in general is shadow IT."},{"id":"q25a","o":"2.5","q":"Fifty accounts each get exactly one failed login using the password \"Winter2026!\". What attack is this?","c":["Password spraying","Brute force","User enumeration","Replay"],"e":"Spraying tries one common password across many accounts to stay under lockout limits. Brute force hammers one account."},{"id":"q25b","o":"2.5","q":"A user logs in from Rapid City and from Moscow ten minutes apart. Which indicator is this?","c":["Impossible travel","Account lockout","Log manipulation","Excessive resource consumption"],"e":"No one can physically travel that fast, so one of the logins is likely an attacker."},{"id":"q25c","o":"2.5","q":"A QR code on a parking-lot flyer leads to a fake company login page. What is this?","c":["Quishing","Smishing","Vishing","Whaling"],"e":"Quishing is QR-code phishing. Smishing is SMS, vishing is voice, whaling targets executives."},{"id":"q25d","o":"2.5","q":"A web request asks for ../../../etc/passwd. Which attack is being tried?","c":["Directory traversal","SQL injection","Buffer overflow","Request forgery"],"e":"The ../ sequences climb out of the web folder to reach other files."},{"id":"q26a","o":"2.6","q":"Hidden text on a web page tells an AI assistant to email the user's files to an outside address. What is this?","c":["Prompt injection","Data poisoning","Hallucination","Bias"],"e":"Prompt injection slips instructions into content the AI reads."},{"id":"q26b","o":"2.6","q":"An attacker slips mislabeled samples into a model's training data so it misses their malware later. What is this?","c":["Poisoning","Jailbreaking","Evasion","Explainability"],"e":"Poisoning corrupts the training data. Evasion fools a model that's already trained."},{"id":"q31a","o":"3.1","q":"A weapons-system network has no physical or wireless connection to any other network. What is this?","c":["Air-gapped network","Microservices","Logical segmentation","Hybrid cloud"],"e":"An air gap is physical separation. Logical segmentation (VLANs) still shares hardware."},{"id":"q31b","o":"3.1","q":"A law requires that citizens' data stays on servers inside the country. Which consideration is this?","c":["Data sovereignty","Data classification","Scalability","Ease of recovery"],"e":"Data sovereignty: data is subject to the laws of the country where it's stored."},{"id":"q32a","o":"3.2","q":"Several servers need to run a service under an account whose password Windows rotates automatically. What should you use?","c":["Group Managed Service Account (gMSA)","Emergency access account","Shared local admin account","Domain admin account"],"e":"gMSAs have long passwords managed and rotated by AD. You create one in Lab 2."},{"id":"q32b","o":"3.2","q":"If the firewall crashes, policy says all traffic must be blocked until it's back. Which failure mode is this?","c":["Fail-closed","Fail-open","Load balancing","Platform diversity"],"e":"Fail-closed favors security. Fail-open favors availability."},{"id":"q32c","o":"3.2","q":"Admins manage switches through a separate network that user traffic never touches. What is this?","c":["Out-of-band management","Tunneling","Security Service Edge","Port mirroring"],"e":"Out-of-band management keeps admin access working, and separate, even if the main network is down or compromised."},{"id":"q33a","o":"3.3","q":"A help desk screen shows only the last four digits of a Social Security number. Which method is this?","c":["Masking","Tokenization","Hashing","Encryption"],"e":"Masking hides part of the data on display. Tokenization swaps the whole value for a stand-in."},{"id":"q33b","o":"3.3","q":"Who handles day-to-day care of data, such as backups and applying access controls?","c":["Data custodian","Data owner","Data controller","Data subprocessor"],"e":"The owner is accountable and sets rules. The custodian does the hands-on work."},{"id":"q33c","o":"3.3","q":"Data loaded in RAM while an application processes it is in which state?","c":["In use","At rest","In transit","Archived"],"e":"At rest is stored, in transit is moving across a network, in use is being processed."},{"id":"q34a","o":"3.4","q":"Leadership says they can lose at most 4 hours of data. Which metric is that?","c":["RPO","RTO","MTTR","MTBF"],"e":"RPO is how much data you can lose, which sets backup frequency. RTO is how long you can be down."},{"id":"q34b","o":"3.4","q":"A backup site has power, network, and hardware ready, but data must be restored before it can take over. Which type is it?","c":["Warm site","Hot site","Cold site","Mirror site"],"e":"Hot is ready immediately, warm needs data restored, cold is mostly an empty room."},{"id":"q41a","o":"4.1","q":"An AD account named svc-backup-old is never used. Any login to it triggers an alert. What is it?","c":["Canary account","Service account","Emergency access account","gMSA"],"e":"A canary account (a kind of honeytoken) exists only to catch someone using it."},{"id":"q41b","o":"4.1","q":"Which standard makes a device authenticate before a switch port lets it onto the network?","c":["802.1X","DMARC","SNMP","SAML"],"e":"802.1X is port-based network access control."},{"id":"q41c","o":"4.1","q":"Which DNS record lists the mail servers allowed to send email for a domain?","c":["SPF","DKIM","DMARC","BIMI"],"e":"SPF lists allowed senders, DKIM signs messages, DMARC says what to do when they fail, BIMI shows a logo."},{"id":"q41d","o":"4.1","q":"Which firewall type inspects HTTP traffic to block attacks like SQL injection against a website?","c":["Web application firewall (WAF)","Layer 4 firewall","NIDS","Content filter"],"e":"A WAF understands web requests at Layer 7. A Layer 4 firewall only sees ports and addresses."},{"id":"q42a","o":"4.2","q":"Before old drives leave the building, IT gets a certificate proving the data was destroyed. Which asset stage is this?","c":["Disposal/decommissioning","Acquisition/procurement","Assignment/accounting","Monitoring/tracking"],"e":"Proof of destruction is part of disposal."},{"id":"q42b","o":"4.2","q":"A laptop is issued to a new hire, and IT records the serial number against their name. Which asset management stage is this?","c":["Assignment/accounting","Planning/scoping","Disposal/decommissioning","Acquisition/procurement"],"e":"Assignment and accounting tie each asset to an owner, so you know who has what."},{"id":"q43a","o":"4.3","q":"After patching, the team rescans to make sure the finding is really gone. Which step is this?","c":["Verification","Remediation","Prioritization","Identification"],"e":"Fixing is remediation. Confirming the fix worked is verification. Lab 5's rescan is this."},{"id":"q43b","o":"4.3","q":"A company pays outside researchers who report vulnerabilities to it. What is this?","c":["Bug bounty program","Gap analysis","Internal audit","Tabletop exercise"],"e":"Bug bounties reward external reporting."},{"id":"q44a","o":"4.4","q":"Which protocol do network devices commonly use to send log messages to a central server?","c":["syslog","NetFlow","SCAP","LDAP"],"e":"syslog ships log messages. NetFlow describes traffic flows. SCAP is for compliance checks."},{"id":"q44b","o":"4.4","q":"An IDS needs a copy of all traffic passing through a switch. What do you configure?","c":["Port mirroring","NetFlow","Tunneling","Load balancing"],"e":"Port mirroring (SPAN) copies traffic to the monitoring port."},{"id":"q44c","o":"4.4","q":"Which standard does DoD's SCC use to run automated configuration compliance checks?","c":["SCAP","SIEM","DLP","SOAR"],"e":"SCAP is the Security Content Automation Protocol, which you use in Lab 5."},{"id":"q45a","o":"4.5","q":"An admin gets domain admin rights for two hours after approval, then they expire automatically. Which model is this?","c":["Just-in-time access","Role-based access","Mandatory access","Discretionary access"],"e":"JIT grants privilege only when needed and for a limited time."},{"id":"q45b","o":"4.5","q":"Access is decided by labels like Secret and Top Secret that users can't change. Which model is this?","c":["Mandatory access control","Discretionary access control","Rule-based access control","Time-based access control"],"e":"MAC uses labels set by the system. With DAC, the owner decides."},{"id":"q45c","o":"4.5","q":"A photo app gets permission to read your cloud storage without ever seeing your password. Which protocol makes that possible?","c":["OAuth","LDAP","802.1X","Kerberos"],"e":"OAuth delegates authorization with tokens."},{"id":"q45d","o":"4.5","q":"A user who changed departments still has access from their old job. What is this called?","c":["Privilege creep","Least privilege","Federation","Identity proofing"],"e":"Access builds up over time. Access reviews catch it."},{"id":"q46a","o":"4.6","q":"A script runs nightly, compares servers to a baseline, and fixes anything that drifted. Which use case is this?","c":["Desired state management","Ticket management","Anomaly detection","User provisioning"],"e":"Desired state means defining how systems should look and enforcing it automatically."},{"id":"q46b","o":"4.6","q":"One bad change in an automation script could push a misconfiguration to every server at once. What should be built in?","c":["Guardrails","More automation","Faster deployment","A larger budget"],"e":"Guardrails (approvals, testing, limits) reduce automation's process risk."},{"id":"q47a","o":"4.7","q":"Training, writing playbooks, and running tabletop exercises belong to which incident response phase?","c":["Preparation","Identification","Containment","Post-incident"],"e":"All of these happen before an incident."},{"id":"q47b","o":"4.7","q":"An analyst unplugs an infected laptop from the network but leaves it powered on. Which phase is this?","c":["Containment","Eradication","Recovery","Identification"],"e":"Containment stops the spread. Leaving it on preserves memory evidence."},{"id":"q47c","o":"4.7","q":"A form records every person who handled a seized drive, and when. What is it?","c":["Chain of custody","Legal hold","E-discovery","Root cause analysis"],"e":"Chain of custody proves evidence wasn't tampered with."},{"id":"q48a","o":"4.8","q":"You need to know which internal hosts talked to a bad IP and how much data moved, without full packets. Which source?","c":["NetFlow","Packet capture","Memory dump","Vulnerability scan"],"e":"NetFlow records who talked to whom and how much, without the content."},{"id":"q48b","o":"4.8","q":"You suspect fileless malware that lives only in RAM. What should you collect?","c":["Memory dump","Bit-level copy of the disk","Firewall logs","Vulnerability scan"],"e":"Fileless malware may never touch the disk, so capture memory."},{"id":"q51a","o":"5.1","q":"A step-by-step document for restoring a domain controller from backup is best called a:","c":["Runbook","Policy","Guideline","Standard"],"e":"Runbooks and SOPs are procedures. Policies say what and why, not how."},{"id":"q51b","o":"5.1","q":"Employees sign a document stating how they may use company computers and networks. What is it?","c":["Acceptable use policy (AUP)","Service-level agreement","Non-disclosure agreement","Memorandum of understanding"],"e":"The AUP sets the rules for using IT resources."},{"id":"q52a","o":"5.2","q":"A $50,000 server loses 20% of its value per incident, and incidents happen once every two years. What is the ALE?","c":["$5,000","$10,000","$25,000","$2,500"],"e":"SLE = $50,000 × 0.20 = $10,000. ARO = 0.5. ALE = $10,000 × 0.5 = $5,000."},{"id":"q52b","o":"5.2","q":"The company buys cyber insurance. Which risk treatment is this?","c":["Transfer","Accept","Avoid","Mitigate"],"e":"Insurance moves the financial impact to someone else."},{"id":"q52c","o":"5.2","q":"After all controls are in place, some risk remains. What is it called?","c":["Residual risk","Risk appetite","Risk register","Inherent risk"],"e":"Residual risk is what's left after mitigation."},{"id":"q53a","o":"5.3","q":"Which agreement sets a vendor's 99.9% uptime and the penalties for missing it?","c":["SLA","MOU","NDA","SOW"],"e":"An SLA defines measurable service levels."},{"id":"q53b","o":"5.3","q":"Which contract clause lets a customer inspect a vendor's security practices?","c":["Right to audit","Due diligence","Rules of engagement","Conflict of interest"],"e":"The right to audit gives the customer inspection rights."},{"id":"q54a","o":"5.4","q":"A lawsuit is coming, so the company must stop deleting related emails. What is this?","c":["Legal hold","Right to be forgotten","Opt-out","Attestation"],"e":"A legal hold overrides normal retention and deletion."},{"id":"q54b","o":"5.4","q":"Under privacy law, which party decides why and how personal data is processed?","c":["Data controller","Data processor","Data custodian","Data steward"],"e":"The controller decides. The processor acts on the controller's behalf."},{"id":"q55a","o":"5.5","q":"Pen testers get full network diagrams and admin credentials before starting. Which type of test is this?","c":["Known environment","Unknown environment","Partially known environment","Passive reconnaissance"],"e":"Known environment, formerly called white box, means full information."},{"id":"q55b","o":"5.5","q":"Which framework catalogs real attacker tactics and techniques, such as T1059 command and scripting interpreter?","c":["MITRE ATT&CK","Cyber Kill Chain","Diamond Model","CVSS"],"e":"ATT&CK is a technique catalog. The Kill Chain is a sequence of phases."},{"id":"q55c","o":"5.5","q":"A team compares its current controls to a framework to find what's missing. What is this?","c":["Gap analysis","Penetration test","Attestation","Sampling"],"e":"A gap analysis shows the distance between where you are and where you need to be."},{"id":"q56a","o":"5.6","q":"A user who clicked a simulated phishing link is automatically assigned a short training module. Which type of training is this?","c":["Corrective","Initial/onboarding","Ongoing","One-to-many"],"e":"Corrective training responds to a specific mistake."},{"id":"q56b","o":"5.6","q":"Accounts payable gets an email from a \"vendor\" asking to change the bank account for future invoices. What is this most likely?","c":["Business email compromise","Smishing","Watering hole","Quishing"],"e":"BEC targets payments and often uses a hijacked or look-alike vendor account."},{"id":"pc1","o":"code","q":"PowerShell. What prints?\n\n[math]::Round(48534876160 / 1GB, 1)","c":["45.2","48.5","45.20","48534876160"],"e":"1GB is 1,073,741,824 bytes, so 48,534,876,160 / 1GB = 45.2017…, rounded to 45.2."},{"id":"pc2","o":"code","q":"PowerShell. What prints?\n\n$list = @('DC01','WS01','UB01')\n$list.Count","c":["3","2","DC01","@('DC01','WS01','UB01')"],"e":"@() makes an array, and .Count is how many items are in it."},{"id":"pc3","o":"code","q":"PowerShell. What prints?\n\n$h = @{dc = 'DC01'}\n\"Server: $($h.dc)\"","c":["Server: DC01","Server: $($h.dc)","Server: dc","Server: @{dc=DC01}"],"e":"Inside double quotes, $( ) runs the expression and drops the result into the string."},{"id":"pc4","o":"code","q":"Bash. What prints?\n\nx=85\nif [ $x -gt 80 ]; then echo WARNING; else echo OK; fi","c":["WARNING","OK","85","Nothing; it's a syntax error"],"e":"-gt means greater than, and 85 is greater than 80."},{"id":"pc5","o":"code","q":"Bash. What prints?\n\necho \"/dev/sda1 85%\" | awk '{print $2}' | tr -d '%'","c":["85","85%","/dev/sda1","sda1 85"],"e":"awk prints the 2nd field (85%), then tr -d deletes the % sign."},{"id":"pc6","o":"code","q":"Bash. You run ./healthcheck.sh and get \"Permission denied.\" ls -l shows -rw-rw-r--. What fixes it?","c":["chmod +x healthcheck.sh","sudo rm healthcheck.sh","chown root healthcheck.sh","mv healthcheck.sh healthcheck"],"e":"There's no x (execute) bit. You hit this exact bug in October."},{"id":"pc7","o":"code","q":"PowerShell. How many services does this return?\n\nGet-Service | Where-Object { $_.Status -eq 'Running' -and $_.StartType -eq 'Automatic' }","c":["Only services that are BOTH running and set to Automatic","Every running service","Every Automatic service","Services that are running OR set to Automatic"],"e":"-and means both conditions must be true. Your health check used the opposite: Automatic AND not running."}],"PBQS":[{"id":"pbq1","o":"1.1","type":"match","title":"Match each control to its type","prompt":"Choose the control type for each example.","opts":["Preventive","Deterrent","Detective","Corrective","Compensating","Directive"],"rows":[["Firewall rule blocking Telnet (port 23)","Preventive"],["Motion camera that alerts the guard desk","Detective"],["Restoring files from backup after ransomware","Corrective"],["Sign: \"Premises under video surveillance\"","Deterrent"],["Extra logging because MFA can't be enabled","Compensating"],["Policy requiring screens to lock when unattended","Directive"]]},{"id":"pbq2","o":"4.7","type":"order","title":"Put the incident response phases in order","prompt":"Use the arrows to order these from first to last, as SY0-801 lists them.","items":["Preparation","Identification","Investigation","Containment","Eradication and recovery","Post-incident (lessons learned)"]},{"id":"pbq3","o":"4.1","type":"match","title":"Match each service to its port","prompt":"Pick the default port for each service.","opts":["22","53","88","443","445","514","636","3389"],"rows":[["SSH","22"],["DNS","53"],["Kerberos","88"],["HTTPS","443"],["SMB","445"],["syslog","514"],["LDAPS","636"],["RDP","3389"]]},{"id":"pbq4","o":"5.2","type":"match","title":"Match each action to its risk treatment","prompt":"Choose the treatment each action represents.","opts":["Transfer","Accept","Avoid","Mitigate"],"rows":[["Buy cyber insurance","Transfer"],["Stop offering the risky service entirely","Avoid"],["Patch the vulnerable servers","Mitigate"],["Document it and do nothing, since fixing costs more than the loss","Accept"]]},{"id":"pbq5","o":"4.8","type":"match","title":"Pick the best data source for each investigation","prompt":"Which source would you check first?","opts":["Windows Security log","NetFlow","Memory dump","Physical access logs","Vulnerability scan","Packet capture"],"rows":[["An account locked out after many failed logins","Windows Security log"],["A large outbound transfer at 3 AM to an unknown IP","NetFlow"],["Suspected fileless malware","Memory dump"],["Who entered the server room last night","Physical access logs"],["Which servers are missing a critical patch","Vulnerability scan"],["The exact contents of a suspicious HTTP request","Packet capture"]]},{"id":"pbq6","o":"4.1","type":"match","title":"Match each email control to what it does","prompt":"Choose the control that does each job.","opts":["SPF","DKIM","DMARC","BIMI"],"rows":[["Lists which servers may send mail for the domain","SPF"],["Adds a cryptographic signature to each message","DKIM"],["Tells receivers what to do when checks fail, and sends reports","DMARC"],["Displays the brand's logo next to verified mail","BIMI"]]}],"ACR":"AAA|Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting\nALE|Annualized Loss Expectancy\nAML/CTF|Anti-money Laundering/Counter-terrorism Financing\nAPT|Advanced Persistent Threat\nARO|Annualized Rate of Occurrence\nAUP|Acceptable Use Policy\nBEC|Business Email Compromise\nBIMI|Brand Indicators for Message Identification\nBIOS|Basic Input/Output System\nBYOD|Bring Your Own Device\nCAB|Change Advisory Board\nCAPTCHA|Completely Automated Public Turing test to tell Computers and Humans Apart\nCI/CD|Continuous Integration and Continuous Deployment\nCIA|Confidentiality, Integrity, and Availability\nCRL|Certificate Revocation List\nCSPM|Cloud Security Posture Management\nCSR|Certificate Signing Request\nCVE|Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures\nCVSS|Common Vulnerability Scoring System\nCWE|Common Weakness Enumeration\nDDoS|Distributed Denial of Service\nDHCP|Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol\nDKIM|DomainKeys Identified Mail\nDLP|Data Loss Prevention\nDMARC|Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting, and Conformance\nDNS|Domain Name System\nEDR|Endpoint Detection and Response\nEOI|Expression of Interest\nGBIC|Gigabit Interface Converter\ngMSA|Group Managed Service Account\nHIDS|Host-based Intrusion Detection System\nHIPS|Host-based Intrusion Prevention System\nIaC|Infrastructure as Code\nIDS|Intrusion Detection System\nIPFIX|Internet Protocol Flow Information Export\nIPS|Intrusion Prevention System\nIPSec|Internet Protocol Security\nLDAP|Lightweight Directory Access Protocol\nLLM|Large Language Model\nLMS|Learning Management System\nMDM|Mobile Device Management\nMFA|Multifactor Authentication\nMOA|Memorandum of Agreement\nMOU|Memorandum of Understanding\nMSA|Master Service Agreement\nMTBF|Mean Time Between Failures\nMTTR|Mean Time to Repair\nNDA|Non-disclosure Agreement\nNFC|Near-field Communication\nNIC|Network Interface Card\nNIDS|Network-based Intrusion Detection System\nNIPS|Network-based Intrusion Prevention System\nNVD|National Vulnerability Database\nOAuth|Open Authorization\nOCSP|Online Certificate Status Protocol\nOT|Operational Technology\nOTP|One-time Password\nPIR|Post-incident Reporting\nPKI|Public Key Infrastructure\nRCS|Rich Communication Services\nRF|Radio Frequency\nRFC|Request for Comments\nRFI|Request for Information\nRFP|Request for Proposal\nRFQ|Request for Quote\nROI|Return on Investment\nRPO|Recovery Point Objective\nRPS|Redundant Power Supply\nRTF|Rich Text Format\nRTO|Recovery Time Objective\nSaaS|Software as a Service\nSAML|Security Assertions Markup Language\nSCAP|Security Content Automation Protocol\nSELinux|Security-enhanced Linux\nSFP|Small Form-factor Pluggable\nSIEM|Security Information and Event Management\nSLE|Single Loss Expectancy\nSNMP|Simple Network Management Protocol\nSOAR|Security Orchestration, Automation, and Response\nSOC|Security Operations Center\nSOP|Standard Operating Procedure\nSOW|Statement of Work\nSPF|Sender Policy Framework\nSSE|Security Service Edge\nSSO|Single Sign-on\nTLS|Transport Layer Security\nTOC|Time-of-check\nTOU|Time-of-use\nUPS|Uninterruptible Power Supply\nUTM|Unified Threat Management\nVNC|Virtual Network Computing\nVPN|Virtual Private Network\nWAF|Web Application Firewall\nWIPS|Wireless Intrusion Prevention System\nXDR|Extended Detection and Response","PORTS":[["22","SSH / SFTP"],["23","Telnet (never use it)"],["25","SMTP"],["53","DNS"],["67/68","DHCP"],["80","HTTP"],["88","Kerberos"],["123","NTP"],["161/162","SNMP"],["389","LDAP"],["443","HTTPS"],["445","SMB"],["514","syslog"],["636","LDAPS"],["3389","RDP"],["5900","VNC"]],"EVENTS":[["4624","Successful logon"],["4625","Failed logon"],["4634","Logoff"],["4720","User account created"],["4726","User account deleted"],["4728","Member added to a global security group"],["4732","Member added to a local security group"],["4740","Account locked out"],["4688","New process created"],["7045","New service installed"],["1102","Security audit log cleared"],["Sysmon 1","Process creation (with command line and parent)"],["Sysmon 3","Network connection"]],"DOMAINS":{"1":"General Security Concepts","2":"Threats, Vulnerabilities, and Attacks","3":"Security Architecture","4":"Security Operations","5":"Program Management and Oversight"},"DRILLS":[{"id":"d1","lang":"PowerShell","p":"Unlock the AD account jsmith.","ok":["^unlock-adaccount (-identity )?['\"]?jsmith['\"]?$"],"a":"Unlock-ADAccount -Identity jsmith","why":"Tier 1 help desk does this all day. Lab 2."},{"id":"d2","lang":"PowerShell","p":"List every locked-out account in the domain.","ok":["^search-adaccount -lockedout$"],"a":"Search-ADAccount -LockedOut","why":"Find them before the phone rings."},{"id":"d3","lang":"PowerShell","p":"Disable the AD account jsmith (offboarding).","ok":["^disable-adaccount (-identity )?['\"]?jsmith['\"]?$"],"a":"Disable-ADAccount -Identity jsmith","why":"Deprovisioning, Sec+ 4.5."},{"id":"d4","lang":"PowerShell","p":"Show user jsmith with ALL of their AD properties.","ok":["^get-aduser (-identity )?['\"]?jsmith['\"]? -properties \\*$"],"a":"Get-ADUser -Identity jsmith -Properties *","why":"By default Get-ADUser shows only a few properties."},{"id":"d5","lang":"cmd","p":"Show which Group Policy objects applied to the current user and computer.","ok":["^gpresult \\/r$"],"a":"gpresult /r","why":"Your Lab 2 checkpoint."},{"id":"d6","lang":"cmd","p":"Force Group Policy to refresh right now.","ok":["^gpupdate \\/force$"],"a":"gpupdate /force","why":"So you don't have to wait up to 90 minutes."},{"id":"d7","lang":"PowerShell","p":"Test whether port 3389 is open on 10.0.2.10.","ok":["^test-netconnection (-computername )?10\\.0\\.2\\.10 -port 3389$","^tnc 10\\.0\\.2\\.10 -port 3389$"],"a":"Test-NetConnection -ComputerName 10.0.2.10 -Port 3389","why":"Lab 4: proving your firewall rule works."},{"id":"d8","lang":"PowerShell","p":"Get the 10 most recent failed-logon events (ID 4625) from the Security log.","ok":["^get-winevent -filterhashtable @\\{ ?logname ?= ?['\"]security['\"] ?; ?id ?= ?4625 ?\\} -maxevents 10$","^get-winevent -filterhashtable @\\{ ?id ?= ?4625 ?; ?logname ?= ?['\"]security['\"] ?\\} -maxevents 10$","^get-winevent -maxevents 10 -filterhashtable @\\{ ?logname ?= ?['\"]security['\"] ?; ?id ?= ?4625 ?\\}$"],"a":"Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security'; Id=4625} -MaxEvents 10","why":"Faster than Get-EventLog and works on any log."},{"id":"d9","lang":"PowerShell","p":"Promote this server to a new forest named lab.local.","ok":["^install-addsforest -domainname ['\"]?lab\\.local['\"]?$"],"a":"Install-ADDSForest -DomainName \"lab.local\"","why":"Lab 1."},{"id":"d10","lang":"Bash","p":"Make healthcheck.sh executable.","ok":["^chmod (\\+x|u\\+x|a\\+x|755|750|744|700) (\\.\\/)?healthcheck\\.sh$"],"a":"chmod +x healthcheck.sh","why":"You hit this exact error in October."},{"id":"d11","lang":"Bash","p":"List services that have failed.","ok":["^(sudo )?systemctl --failed$","^(sudo )?systemctl list-units --state=failed$"],"a":"systemctl --failed","why":"Lab 0's Bash step."},{"id":"d12","lang":"Bash","p":"Count lines containing \"Failed password\" in /var/log/auth.log.","ok":["^(sudo )?grep -c ['\"]failed password['\"] \\/var\\/log\\/auth\\.log$","^(sudo )?grep ['\"]failed password['\"] \\/var\\/log\\/auth\\.log \\| wc -l$"],"a":"grep -c \"Failed password\" /var/log/auth.log","why":"Brute-force and spraying evidence, Sec+ 2.5."},{"id":"d13","lang":"Bash","p":"Show disk usage for all mounted filesystems in human-readable sizes.","ok":["^df -h$"],"a":"df -h","why":"The core of your Bash health check."},{"id":"d14","lang":"Bash","p":"Follow the SSH service's log live.","ok":["^(sudo )?journalctl -u ssh(\\.service)? -f$","^(sudo )?journalctl -f -u ssh(\\.service)?$"],"a":"journalctl -u ssh -f","why":"Watch logins happen while you test."},{"id":"d15","lang":"Bash","p":"Join this Ubuntu machine to the lab.local domain as Administrator.","ok":["^(sudo )?realm join (-u administrator )?lab\\.local( -u administrator)?$"],"a":"sudo realm join -U Administrator lab.local","why":"Lab 3."},{"id":"d16","lang":"Bash","p":"Scan 10.0.2.0/24 and detect service versions.","ok":["^(sudo )?nmap -sv 10\\.0\\.2\\.0\\/24$"],"a":"nmap -sV 10.0.2.0/24","why":"Know your own attack surface (Sec+ 2.4). Only scan your own lab."},{"id":"d17","lang":"Bash","p":"Generate a new SSH key pair using the ed25519 algorithm.","ok":["^ssh-keygen -t ed25519( -c .+)?$"],"a":"ssh-keygen -t ed25519","why":"Then switch password logins off."},{"id":"d18","lang":"Git","p":"Stage every changed file in the current folder.","ok":["^git add (\\.|-a|--all)$"],"a":"git add .","why":"Step one of every lab push."}],"LABS":[{"name":"Start here","items":[{"id":"lab0","t":"Lab 0: finish your open scripts","tags":["~1 week"],"d":"You're close on both health checks. Finishing them gives you the first repo to link on applications.","steps":[["lab0a","PowerShell: save the $LogFolder fix so logs land in C:\\Scripts\\Logs"],["lab0b","PowerShell: finish Register-ScheduledTask so it runs daily as SYSTEM"],["lab0c","Bash: add systemctl --failed and failed SSH logins from /var/log/auth.log"],["lab0d","Bash: write output to a dated log file, then schedule it with crontab -e"],["lab0e","Bash: fill the disk with fallocate -l 5G ~/bigfile and confirm the warning fires"],["lab0f","GitHub: publish the sysadmin-health-checks repo with a README comparing both versions"]],"checkpoint":"Both scripts run on a schedule and the repo is public."}]},{"name":"Windows domain","items":[{"id":"lab1","t":"Lab 1: Active Directory domain","tags":["Sec+ 4.5"],"d":"Build a domain controller in VirtualBox, doing as much as you can in PowerShell.","steps":[["lab1a","Download the Windows Server 2025 evaluation ISO"],["lab1b","Create a NAT Network in VirtualBox (File > Tools > Network Manager)"],["lab1c","New VM: 4 GB RAM, 2 CPUs, 60 GB disk, Desktop Experience install"],["lab1d","Rename-Computer DC01, then restart"],["lab1e","Fixed IP 10.0.2.10, gateway 10.0.2.1, DNS 127.0.0.1 (New-NetIPAddress, Set-DnsClientServerAddress)"],["lab1f","Install-WindowsFeature AD-Domain-Services -IncludeManagementTools"],["lab1g","Install-ADDSForest -DomainName \"lab.local\""],["lab1h","Get-ADDomain returns lab.local"],["lab1i","Snapshot named Clean DC"]],"checkpoint":"You can explain why a DC needs a fixed IP. That's a common interview question."},{"id":"lab2","t":"Lab 2: help desk drills","tags":["Sec+ 1.1","4.5","4.6"],"d":"Do what a Tier 1 tech does all day, first by hand, then with scripts.","steps":[["lab2a","Create OUs for Staff, Admins, Service Accounts, Computers"],["lab2b","users.csv with 20 users, then Import-Csv | New-ADUser in a script"],["lab2c","Groups and Add-ADGroupMember (role-based access)"],["lab2d","Password reset (Set-ADAccountPassword) and unlock (Unlock-ADAccount)"],["lab2e","Offboard a user: disable, move to a Disabled OU, strip groups"],["lab2f","GPO: password and lockout policy"],["lab2g","GPO: mapped drive for one group"],["lab2h","GPO: block USB storage"],["lab2i","Windows 11 Enterprise eval VM joined with Add-Computer"],["lab2j","Log in as a domain user and confirm with gpresult /r"],["lab2k","Create one gMSA and run a scheduled task with it"],["lab2l","Extend HealthCheck.ps1 to check NTDS, DNS and Netlogon"]],"checkpoint":"A domain user logs into the client and your GPOs apply."},{"id":"lab3","t":"Lab 3: Linux joins the domain","tags":["Sec+ 4.5"],"d":"Mixed Windows and Linux is what the Ellsworth postings describe.","steps":[["lab3a","Point Ubuntu's DNS at DC01 (10.0.2.10)"],["lab3b","sudo apt install realmd sssd sssd-tools adcli krb5-user packagekit"],["lab3c","realm discover lab.local"],["lab3d","realm join lab.local -U Administrator"],["lab3e","SSH in as a domain user"],["lab3f","Give an AD group sudo rights in /etc/sudoers.d/"]],"checkpoint":"You SSH into Ubuntu with a lab.local account."}]},{"name":"Network and security","items":[{"id":"lab4","t":"Lab 4: networking and firewall","tags":["Sec+ 3.2","4.1","CCNA"],"d":"Packet Tracer first, then a real virtual firewall in your lab.","steps":[["lab4a","Packet Tracer: VLANs and trunking"],["lab4b","Packet Tracer: inter-VLAN routing and DHCP"],["lab4c","Packet Tracer: ACLs and port security"],["lab4d","Packet Tracer: OSPF"],["lab4e","pfSense VM with two internal networks: Users and Servers"],["lab4f","pfSense hands out DHCP on the Users network"],["lab4g","Firewall rule blocking RDP from Users to Servers, proven with Test-NetConnection -Port 3389"],["lab4h","Wireshark capture of a DHCP exchange (Discover, Offer, Request, Ack)"]],"checkpoint":"The client gets DHCP through pfSense, and your rule blocks what you meant it to."},{"id":"lab5","t":"Lab 5: STIG hardening","tags":["Sec+ 2.4","4.3","DoD"],"d":"This is daily work on base, and few entry-level applicants can show it.","links":[["STIG Viewer","https://public.cyber.mil/stigs/srg-stig-tools"],["SCAP tools (SCC)","https://public.cyber.mil/stigs/scap/"]],"steps":[["lab5a","Download STIG Viewer and the SCAP Compliance Checker (SCC)"],["lab5b","Scan DC01 with SCC against the Windows Server benchmark"],["lab5c","Import the results into a STIG Viewer checklist"],["lab5d","Fix 10 findings (CAT I first) with GPO or PowerShell"],["lab5e","Rescan and record the before and after score"],["lab5f","Bonus: scan Ubuntu with OpenSCAP (oscap)"],["lab5g","Push the before and after write-up to GitHub"]],"checkpoint":"A before and after compliance score you can talk through."},{"id":"lab6","t":"Lab 6: logging and detection","tags":["Sec+ 2.5","4.4","4.7"],"d":"See attacks in logs, the way a SOC or sysadmin does.","links":[["Sysmon","https://learn.microsoft.com/sysinternals/downloads/sysmon"],["sysmon-config","https://github.com/SwiftOnSecurity/sysmon-config"],["Wazuh","https://wazuh.com/"]],"steps":[["lab6a","Sysmon on the Windows 11 client with a community config"],["lab6b","A free SIEM on Ubuntu (Wazuh or Splunk Free)"],["lab6c","Agents on DC01 and the client sending logs"],["lab6d","Alert rule for powershell -EncodedCommand"],["lab6e","Run a harmless encoded command and watch the alert fire"],["lab6f","Build your PowerShell watcher: new shells and services, with parent process"],["lab6g","One-page incident report using the 4.7 phases"]],"checkpoint":"An alert fires on your test command, and you've written it up."},{"id":"lab7","t":"Lab 7: patching and recovery","tags":["Sec+ 3.4","1.2"],"d":"Sustainment work: keep systems patched and prove you can bring them back.","steps":[["lab7a","Windows Update settings pushed by GPO"],["lab7b","Windows Server Backup feature installed, backup scheduled"],["lab7c","Enable-ADOptionalFeature 'Recycle Bin Feature'"],["lab7d","Delete a user, then Restore-ADObject"],["lab7e","Delete a file, then restore it from backup and time it (your RTO)"],["lab7f","Write a runbook for both restores"]],"checkpoint":"A runbook someone else could follow."},{"id":"lab8","t":"Lab 8: certificates and encryption","tags":["Sec+ 1.3"],"d":"Add-on lab for the crypto objective.","steps":[["lab8a","Install AD Certificate Services as an Enterprise Root CA"],["lab8b","Publish a certificate template and auto-enroll with GPO"],["lab8c","Generate a CSR on Ubuntu with openssl req, then sign it with your CA"],["lab8d","Turn on BitLocker on the client, with the recovery key saved in AD"],["lab8e","Revoke a certificate and find it in the CRL"]],"checkpoint":"You can explain CSR, CA, CRL and OCSP with your own lab as the example."}]}]};
